---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('shopify-email')!;
const b = appBySlug('yotpo-email-sms')!;
const path = '/compare/shopify-email-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-yotpo-email-sms');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-yotpo-email-sms');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Is Yotpo Email & SMS a current alternative to Shopify Email?', answer: 'Yotpo announced full Email & SMS deprecation scheduled for December 31, 2025. Treat this as a migration decision. Confirm any legacy account or export access directly; old product articles do not establish current availability.' },
  { question: 'Can Shopify replace the old recovery flow?', answer: 'Evaluate the available Shopify Messaging browse, cart and checkout templates against the actual requirements. Rebuild and test eligibility, return links, purchase exits and offer rules. Historical Yotpo flows and reporting do not automatically transfer into native automations.' },
  { question: 'Does the messaging sunset mean removing Yotpo reviews or loyalty?', answer: 'Separate the messaging replacement from reviews and loyalty. Inventory any reward or review data used in recovery and verify the replacement connection. Do not remove an unrelated service simply because the old messaging product was retired.' },
  { question: 'What if legacy exports are unavailable?', answer: 'Inventory the permission and suppression records you still hold. Confirm available retrieval with the supplier and leave unresolved records excluded from new sends. Do not recreate subscription permission from purchase history or assume historical queues and links remain accessible.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name}: moving Shopify recovery forward</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">For merchants replacing legacy Yotpo messaging: decide whether native recovery covers the job, and identify the data and offers that need rebuilding.</p>
    <div class="mt-10"><TldrBox picks={[
      { label: 'Legacy product', tool: b.name, reason: 'Yotpo scheduled full messaging deprecation for December 31, 2025.' },
      { label: 'Native candidate', tool: a.name, reason: 'Evaluate current Shopify Messaging templates against the recovery you still need.' },
      { label: 'Migration priority', tool: 'Permission and continuity', reason: 'Reconcile exclusions, return links and outstanding rewards before enabling sends.' },
    ]} /></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>This is a replacement decision</h2>
      <p>Yotpo's <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">September 2025 migration notice</a> scheduled full deprecation of Email &amp; SMS for December 31, 2025 and named Attentive and Omnisend as migration partners. That date is past. This page therefore does not recommend buying Yotpo Email &amp; SMS as a new recovery sender. The notice does not establish whether a particular legacy account can still retrieve exports, historical reports or templates today; confirm those details with Yotpo.</p>
      <p>Keep the review and loyalty decisions separate. A messaging migration does not by itself require replacing either program. The practical question is which recovery dependencies relied on the former sender: audience fields, reward codes, review content, suppression lists, text permission, and links already delivered to shoppers.</p>
      <h2>What Shopify can be evaluated to replace</h2>
      <p>This route retains the Shopify Email name, while current recovery documentation uses Shopify Messaging. The <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">native automation guide</a> distinguishes browse abandonment, adding to cart without checkout, and starting checkout without ordering. It documents email, SMS and WhatsApp, subject to eligibility and availability. Abandoned-checkout email is outside the email send allowance; checkout texts and WhatsApp messages are charged.</p>
      <p>Choose native recovery when your store's available workflow can express the required entry, eligibility, return destination and exit. Do not equate adding another messaging platform with adding a missing channel until you have checked the native account. Equally, do not assume native templates reproduce a legacy reward segment or historical engagement branch merely because both systems use Shopify order data.</p>
      <h2>Build a replacement map from surviving records</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Flow logic:</strong> list the original trigger, delay, audience filters, offer and purchase exit. Mark evidence as an export, screenshot, staff record or unknown. Reconstruct the business purpose rather than treating remembered timing as a proven optimum.</li>
        <li><strong>Contact state:</strong> reconcile email subscription, text subscription, opt-outs and exclusions separately. Preserve source and timestamp records where held. A Shopify customer record or past order is not proof of permission for a new channel.</li>
        <li><strong>Offers and rewards:</strong> list issued codes, remaining validity, exclusions and any loyalty conditions. Verify whether the replacement can obtain the needed reward data. If it cannot, use a simpler message without promising a live balance or personalized reward.</li>
        <li><strong>Return destinations:</strong> inventory links in old messages and templates. Test whether the shopper reaches the intended basket, variant and offer. Do not copy a legacy provider URL into a new message without confirming what still operates.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For every unknown, name an owner and decide whether it blocks sending or only reporting. Unresolved suppression or channel permission should block the affected audience. A missing historical open metric need not block a newly tested checkout reminder. If exports are unavailable, use records you legitimately retain and supplier assistance; do not manufacture a full migration history.</p>
      <h2>Proposed native acceptance test</h2>
      <p>This is an unexecuted test plan. Use authorized test contacts representing the eligibility states your policy permits, and record events, queued sends and exclusions.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Start with a simple checkout reminder without a new discount. Abandon checkout and inspect its trigger, recipient eligibility and return link. Separately test cart abandonment before checkout so an event-stage mismatch is visible.</li>
        <li>Complete a purchase during the wait. Confirm the intended exit and inspect any other active native or third-party recovery flow for pending messages. A successful send preview does not establish cancellation behavior.</li>
        <li>Test an opt-out and a contact excluded by your migration records. Verify they remain excluded after the initial import and later customer updates. Do not mark all imported contacts subscribed as a shortcut.</li>
        <li>Test one still-valid legacy offer and one expired or ineligible offer. Confirm the message avoids promising redemption that checkout rejects. For loyalty-dependent content, verify the specific data path or keep that personalization inactive.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>When the basic reminder works, add only branches that solve a real customer need: delivery information, sizing help or an existing valid offer. Fix shipping or payment friction at checkout. Keep stock checks and return-link checks in the acceptance record; a reminder for an unavailable variant needs a useful destination rather than false urgency.</p>
      <h2>Restore service without replaying a backlog</h2>
      <p>If recovery has been inactive, do not send a newly enabled flow to every historical abandoned cart. Old carts may have been purchased, changed, expired or opted out. Define a fresh entry boundary, recheck eligibility and inspect what activation does to existing records. Keep newly observed events distinct from imported history.</p>
      <p>If a replacement sender is already active, switching again requires an inventory of that sender as well as the legacy Yotpo records. Pause the chosen old entry, inspect delayed work and name the person responsible for cancellation. Keep rollback tied to a currently available, tested workflow; a discontinued service is not a reliable fallback. Preserve unrelated review and loyalty integrations unless a verified dependency requires changing them.</p>
      <h2>Rebuild the economic baseline</h2>
      <p>Hypothetical example: a $286 basket at a 45% full-price gross margin has $157.30 product cost and $128.70 gross profit. A 15% discount reduces revenue by $42.90 to $243.10. With unchanged product cost, gross profit becomes $85.80 before other variable costs. The old shortcut of discounting the profit figure would understate the offer cost.</p>
      <p>Keep old attributed recovery separate from the new native report: audience, trigger, attribution window and downtime may differ. Reconcile actual Shopify orders, refunds and incentives. Where feasible, use a randomized comparison sized for your traffic to estimate additional profit. Neither an increase after reactivation nor an old provider's revenue total alone proves incremental recovery.</p>
      <p>Compare current channel charges, staff time and any required integration work. There is no verified fixed monthly price or universal incentive cap in this comparison. Sequenzy is a possible later supplier evaluation if another lifecycle requirement emerges; it is not established here as an automatic migration destination or a substitute for recovering permission records.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
